    "q-325130": {
      "dom_id": "step-q325130",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "The fasting glucose level of a clinic’s patients is normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu \\,\\text{mg/dL}$ and a standard deviation of $15\\,\\text{mg/dL}.$ Additionally, $60%$ of the patients have a glucose level between $0.92\\mu \\,\\text{mg/dL}$ and $1.08\\mu \\,\\text{mg/dL}.$ Find the percentage of patients with a fasting glucose lower than $130\\,\\text{mg/dL}.$ **Round your answer to two decimal places.**\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.75\\,$ | $\\,0.80\\,$ | $\\,0.85\\,$ | $\\,0.90\\,$ | $\\,0.95\\,$ | $\\,0.99\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,0.6745\\,$ | $\\,0.8416\\,$ | $\\,1.0364\\,$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.9\\,$ | $\\,0.0262\\,$ | $\\,0.0256\\,$ | $\\,0.0250\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.8\\,$ | $\\,0.0329\\,$ | $\\,0.0322\\,$ | $\\,0.0314\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.7\\,$ | $\\,0.0409\\,$ | $\\,0.0401\\,$ | $\\,0.0392\\,$ |\n\nApproximately\n{{field-1}}$%$ of the patients have a fasting glucose lower than $130\\,\\text{mg/dL}.$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $X$ represent the fasting glucose level of a randomly selected patient. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{15}^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X<130).$ But first, we need to find the mean $\\mu .$ \n\nWe know that $P(0.92\\mu <X<1.08\\mu )=0.60.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.92\\mu <X<1.08\\mu ) & =0.60 \\\\ 2P(\\mu <X<1.08\\mu ) & =0.60 \\\\ P(\\mu <X<1.08\\mu ) & =0.30 \\\\ P(X<\\mu )+P(\\mu <X<1.08\\mu ) & =0.5+0.30 \\\\ P(X<1.08\\mu ) & =0.80\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1.08\\mu ) & =0.80 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{0.08\\mu }{15}) & =0.80 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{0.08\\mu }{15}) & =0.80 \\\\ \\frac{0.08\\mu }{15} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.80).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.80)=0.8416.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{0.08\\mu }{15} & =0.8416 \\\\ 0.08\\mu  & =12.624 \\\\ \\mu  & =\\frac{12.624}{0.08} \\\\  & ≈157.80.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X<130)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<130) & =P(Z<\\frac{130-157.80}{15}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<-1.85) \\\\  & =Φ(-1.85) \\\\  & =0.0322 \\\\  & ≈3.22%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $3.22%$ of the patients have a fasting glucose lower than $130\\,\\text{mg/dL}.$",
